ftrace: record fentry sites with objtool, which writes them as NOPs

Where gcc accepts -mrecord-mcount, Kconfig picks the compiler to record the
__fentry__ call sites, and the compiler leaves them as calls: ftrace_init()
then rewrites every one of them (~41,000 on this kernel) into a NOP at boot.
objtool can record the same sites and write the NOPs at build time
(--mnop, CONFIG_OBJTOOL_NOP_MCOUNT), which x86 supports
(HAVE_OBJTOOL_NOP_MCOUNT), and then ftrace_need_init_nop() is false and the
rewrite does not happen. This makes that path the one taken.

Measured 2026-09-29 on the lab runner (spin-machine boot:initcalls, 15 boots
interleaved, 1 vCPU): -7.0 ms of kernel boot, all of it outside initcalls.
The function tracer, fentry/fexit and kprobe_multi work as before.

Not upstream: Kconfig prefers the compiler path wherever it exists.

--- a/kernel/trace/Kconfig
+++ b/kernel/trace/Kconfig
@@ -883,6 +883,7 @@
 	depends on $(cc-option,-mrecord-mcount)
 	depends on !FTRACE_MCOUNT_USE_PATCHABLE_FUNCTION_ENTRY
 	depends on DYNAMIC_FTRACE
+	depends on !HAVE_OBJTOOL_NOP_MCOUNT
 
 config FTRACE_MCOUNT_USE_OBJTOOL
 	def_bool y
